  "talk": [
    {
      "owner": "D",
      "seconds": 20,
      "script": "Our project asks whether generated training images help when real labels are limited. We built a toolkit that generates class images, trains matched classifiers and checks their performance on real test data. We demonstrate it with twenty real images per class.",
      "sources": [
        "agent.md",
        "configs/study.json"
      ]
    },
    {
      "owner": "A",
      "seconds": 35,
      "script": "The input is a class list and a small real training set. Stable Diffusion generates candidates. We select images, extract frozen features and train linear classifiers. The outputs include saved models, predictions and compute records. Our recorded run produced two thousand candidates and fifteen classifiers. The release preserves the data and settings needed to audit the result.",
      "sources": [
        "shared/synthetic_v2/README.md",
        "shared/synthetic_v2/g4/README.md",
        "https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.10752"
      ]
    },
    {
      "owner": "B",
      "seconds": 45,
      "script": "We compare real data alone with fifty or one hundred and fifty synthetic images per class, selected randomly or by CLIP. Three paired seeds share real samples, head initialization and real sampling. Every head uses five hundred updates. We freeze ResNet and BatchNorm. Mixed training gives equal weight to real and synthetic losses. This evaluates that mixing strategy, including its changed real-loss weight.",
      "sources": [
        "configs/study.json",
        "code/classifier.py",
        "report/B_v04_review.md"
      ]
    },
    {
      "owner": "C",
      "seconds": 45,
      "script": "On ten thousand real test images, the baseline reaches seventy-one point four four percent. The mixed conditions reach about seventy-five percent. Their average improvements range from three point six two to three point eight four percentage points. All three paired seeds improve. These results support a benefit for this classifier and setting. The displayed standard deviations describe seed variation, not confidence intervals.",
      "sources": [
        "shared/synthetic_v2/c_evaluation/tables/07_g4_summary.csv",
        "report/C_results_evaluation.md"
      ]
    },
    {
      "owner": "C",
      "seconds": 30,
      "script": "CLIP selection adds only about zero point two percentage points over random selection. Keeping one hundred and fifty instead of fifty adds almost nothing. Recorded stages total about forty-eight minutes, dominated by generation. Both sizes share the same generated pool, so retaining fewer images does not save that generation cost. These small differences remain descriptive.",
      "sources": [
        "shared/synthetic_v2/c_evaluation/tables/13_g4_direct_comparisons.csv",
        "shared/synthetic_v2/c_evaluation/tables/12_g4_cost_summary.csv"
      ]
    },
    {
      "owner": "C",
      "seconds": 35,
      "script": "A useful tool must also reveal stronger alternatives. Zero-shot CLIP reaches eighty-eight point two one percent here. It uses a different pretrained model, so this is an alternative approach rather than a controlled augmentation comparison. Our result supports improving the fixed ResNet baseline. It does not establish the best practical classifier for this benchmark.",
      "sources": [
        "report/C_results_evaluation.md",
        "https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/radford21a.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "owner": "D",
      "seconds": 35,
      "script": "Two annotation forms agree that the target class is recognizable in all one hundred sampled images. Structural defects can still remain. Author testing passes the functional checks, but exposes a reading problem: the numbers are visible while their meaning is unclear. We added condition definitions and separate loss terms. External user usability remains untested.",
      "sources": [
        "shared/synthetic_v2/audits/human_annotations/README.md",
        "shared/synthetic_v2/audits/author_self_test/README.md"
      ]
    },
    {
      "owner": "D",
      "seconds": 25,
      "script": "Our contribution is a reproducible toolkit and a controlled case study. Prior work already studies synthetic augmentation. Our evidence covers one dataset, one label budget and a shared generation pool. The team divides engineering, classification, evaluation and product delivery as shown. The toolkit helps inspect both the gains and the limits.",
      "sources": [
        "分工.md",
        "progress.md",
        "https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.07574"
      ]
    }
  ],
